<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attalus II Philadelphus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">220–138 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attalus II Philadelphus was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> from 159 to 138 BCE, succeeding his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a>. A loyal ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he fought in its wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Attalia</a> (modern Antalya), rebuilt the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the Stoa of Attalos in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">Agora</a>. He was succeeded by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attalus-ii-philadelphus.jpg" alt="Attalus II Philadelphus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer: Cobija. Sculptor: Meretguly Ouezov in March 2004, per [1] · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:II._Attalos_Heykeli_detay.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
